00:01:02.331 --> 00:01:04.077
♪ Miss independent ♪

00:01:04.077 --> 00:01:05.370
(Neil laughs)
♪ Misunderstood ♪

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:08.340
- In fact, the full
interview, because for this,

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.910
we cut and paste, for
this, the full interview

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:13.330
is available on the
StarTalk YouTube channel.

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:14.420
- Very nice.
- Alright.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:16.800
And her newest album,
which is how she landed

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:20.080
in our universe, is called
The Meaning of Life.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.710
- Oh!
- That's how this happened.

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:24.870
- Let me tell ya, if Kelly
Clarkson is telling me

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:27.168
the meaning of life, I don't wanna live.

00:01:27.168 --> 00:01:30.080
(Neil laughs)

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:30.913
I'm just sayin'.

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:36.080
- So we have, in-studio,
expert guest because I

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:41.080
am no vocologist or
vocal, I sing really good

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:42.785
in the shower.

00:01:42.785 --> 00:01:45.970
- Now, your speaking voice
would betray the fact

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:48.360
that you might be able to sing well.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:49.260
- In the shower.
- Oh, okay.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:50.350
- That's it.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:53.602
The acoustics in the shower
make anybody sound good.

00:01:53.602 --> 00:01:58.602
Our guest today is professor
and performer Brian Gill.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:00.330
Welcome to the show, Brian!

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:01.757
- Hey, thank you very
much, glad to be here.

00:02:01.757 --> 00:02:04.210
- So, you are, I got here, you are

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:07.930
associate professor of voice.

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:08.930
- Yes.
- That's a thing?

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:10.600
- Yes, and voice pedagogy.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.386
- And voice pedagogy!
- Yes.

00:02:12.386 --> 00:02:14.430
- Ooh, wow.
- At Indiana University.

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:15.542
- [Brian] Yes.

00:02:15.542 --> 00:02:17.130
- This is in Bloomington, Indiana.

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:18.000
- [Brian] Bloomington, Indiana, yes.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.120
A lovely little college town.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:20.627
- The Jacob School of Music.
- Yes.

00:02:20.627 --> 00:02:22.090
- [Neil] That's a beautiful thing.

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:22.930
- Yeah.
- And it says here,

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:24.720
you are a vocologist.
- Yes.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:26.310
- Vocologist!
- That's just wrong.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:27.369
That should not be a word.

00:02:27.369 --> 00:02:28.260
(all laugh)

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:29.470
- They made that up.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:30.590
- You made that up!

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:32.290
- They did it real quick, on the fly.

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:33.590
- I'm sorry, I won't be in this Friday.

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:35.622
I have an appointment with my vocologist.

00:02:35.622 --> 00:02:36.930
- Yeah, yeah.
- You're fired.

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:38.940
- It sounds official so
then people believe you.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:39.773
- It does!

00:02:39.773 --> 00:02:40.700
Just because you're good at a thing

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:42.430
doesn't mean you put -ologist on it and,

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:43.848
that's another thing.

00:02:43.848 --> 00:02:45.638
- Are you a voice teacher or a vocologist?

00:02:45.638 --> 00:02:47.388
- I'm a funnyologist.

00:02:48.725 --> 00:02:50.602
- Comedologist.
- Got it right.

00:02:50.602 --> 00:02:52.070
Comedologist.
- I'll laugh, then.

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:53.920
- So, you're a tenor.
- Yes.

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:56.430
- And you're a performer, as a tenor,

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:59.280
and opera, musical theater,
love musical theater.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.220
And concert recitals in
the US and in Europe.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:04.430
I feel privileged that you're in town.

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:06.530
- I feel privileged to
be here with you, too.

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:07.750
- [Neil] We snatched you out of,

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:09.750
you were gonna head back
to Indiana yesterday.

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:11.160
- Yes, I'm going back tonight.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:14.647
- And we delayed your plane
knowing that you would come.

00:03:14.647 --> 00:03:15.750
So, I gotta know--

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:16.963
- Trouble on the tarmac?

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:20.330
- I gotta know, I got people.

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:22.360
I know the guy who--
- Stop him.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:23.575
- Checks the engines.

00:03:23.575 --> 00:03:26.260
- You see that dude out
there with the two cones,

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:27.730
the lit up cones?
- Yeah!

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:30.300
- Those are Neil's drags.
- They dragged me off.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:31.133
I didn't know what hit me.

00:03:31.133 --> 00:03:32.910
- So, I have to ask.

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:35.094
What specifically is vocology?

00:03:35.094 --> 00:03:38.850
- It's, quick definition,
science of practice

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:42.560
and voice habilitation, and
if necessary rehabilitation.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:43.393
- Gotcha.
- Oh!

00:03:43.393 --> 00:03:45.860
So, now I know what habilitation is.

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:46.693
I never knew.

00:03:46.693 --> 00:03:47.800
We all know what rehabilitation is.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:48.633
- Of course.
- Yep.

00:03:48.633 --> 00:03:50.440
- But nobody ever habilitates.
- Truly.

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:51.610
- Only in private.
- Yeah.

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:53.770
- I do habilitate.
- Fix it later.

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:54.603
Wait 'till it's broken.

00:03:54.603 --> 00:03:57.758
- Wait, so habilitate is the,
what would that be, then?

00:03:57.758 --> 00:03:59.903
The sustenance and care and
feeding of your voice, right?

00:03:59.903 --> 00:04:02.760
- Yes, you teach a person
high level functions

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:04.159
so you don't injure your voice.

00:04:04.159 --> 00:04:05.158
- So you don't injure it.
- Yes.

00:04:05.158 --> 00:04:06.260
- [Neil] Then you don't
have to rehabilitate.

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:07.920
- That's right, because
it's more difficult

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:10.018
to rehabilitate than habilitate.

00:04:10.018 --> 00:04:12.300
Once the system's
broken, to get the pieces

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:14.910
back together, including
the mind, and how the person

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:17.500
reacted to the injury or
the breakdown of the system

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:18.640
is very difficult.

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:20.317
- Wow.
- That's neat.

00:04:20.317 --> 00:04:21.700
- So you know you're a
real singer when you have

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:25.600
a singing injury.
(Neil laughs)

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:27.530
I would love to be in this opera but I'm

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:28.623
in injured reserve!

00:04:31.427 --> 00:04:32.260
Right, right.

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:33.610
- I tore the rotator cuff in my voice!

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:36.010
- Now playing the part
of Carmen, unfortunately,

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:42.223
due to injury, I'm out for the season.

00:04:43.306 --> 00:04:44.910
- He's out for the season!
- Right.

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:47.697
- Yes, that's what happens.

00:04:47.697 --> 00:04:49.810
And it covers any professional voice user,

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:51.790
which would also be someone like you all

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:54.450
that use your voice in this medium, so.

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:55.598
- We do.
- That's right.

00:04:55.598 --> 00:04:58.720
So if something happens with
a politician or a teacher

00:04:58.720 --> 00:04:59.810
at a school, or something like that,

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:01.250
because they have a heavy voice load,

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:03.050
then a vocologist is uniquely trained

00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:03.883
to help them out.

00:05:03.883 --> 00:05:05.910
- I remember Bill Clinton.
- Yes!

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:07.890
- I remember he needed the, yeah.

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:11.310
And he ran into, he
already sounded like this

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:14.450
to begin with, yet I
just wanna let you know

00:05:14.450 --> 00:05:19.166
that I talk like this
because I'm not habilitated

00:05:19.166 --> 00:05:24.166
the way I should, and then
I remember he was either

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:27.880
campaigning for himself or
Hillary or Barack Obama,

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:28.713
I'm not sure.

00:05:28.713 --> 00:05:31.350
But either way, he had to
come off the campaign trail

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:32.183
because--
- He lost his voice.

00:05:32.183 --> 00:05:34.883
- He had injured his voice,
and they called it an injury.

00:05:34.883 --> 00:05:38.058
- Yeah, yeah, a really
common thing, unfortunately.

00:05:38.058 --> 00:05:39.059
- Okay, so--
- Wow.

00:05:39.059 --> 00:05:39.892
- But, if you're habilitated--

00:05:39.892 --> 00:05:40.910
- Interesting.
- Nice.

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:43.977
- Okay, we'll be drawing
on your expertise heavily

00:05:43.977 --> 00:05:47.432
for this conversation that I've had

00:05:47.432 --> 00:05:48.910
with Kelly Clarkson.

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:52.040
She's been judged as one
of the best vocalists

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:53.372
in the industry.

00:05:53.372 --> 00:05:55.384
- I would say so, yeah.
- Definitely.

00:05:55.384 --> 00:05:56.615
- She's got a powerhouse voice.

00:05:56.615 --> 00:05:57.710
- Mhm.
- She does.

00:05:57.710 --> 00:05:59.333
- She's versatile.
- Mhm.

00:05:59.333 --> 00:06:01.650
- Sold 25 million albums.
- Wow!

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:04.000
- 36 million singles, worldwide.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:05.990
- Geez.
- And she was the first artist

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:08.530
in history, I got a crack
team of researchers here,

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:12.700
to top each of Billboard's
Pop, Adult Contemporary,

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:14.810
Country, and Dance charts.

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:15.680
- Wow.
- Man.

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:17.980
- And only two of those actually count.

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:18.853
- Which ones?

00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:20.870
Pop and Dance, what?

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:22.930
Right.
- No country, country people--

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:25.420
- Country right now is huge.
- Yeah, country's huge.

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:26.253
- Yeah, I'm joking.

00:06:26.253 --> 00:06:27.167
What do I know?

00:06:27.167 --> 00:06:28.029
I'm jokin'.
- Hootie went over there.

00:06:28.029 --> 00:06:30.170
- So, I had to ask her
about her newest album,

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:31.590
The Meaning of Life.
- Nice!

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:35.190
- So just, straight up, let's figure out

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:36.023
where that came from.

00:06:36.023 --> 00:06:36.856
Check it out.

00:06:36.856 --> 00:06:39.130
So, The Meaning of Life
is your eighth album.

00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:41.420
- Yes.
- And forgive me,

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:42.790
I haven't heard all your albums--

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:44.130
- Yes.
- But I heard this one.

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:45.960
- I'm so offended.
(both laugh)

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:47.401
- I've listened--
- I'm leaving.

00:06:47.401 --> 00:06:49.061
(both laugh)

00:06:49.061 --> 00:06:51.060
Just kidding, I'm just kidding!

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:54.890
- But, this one, I've heard
the stuff that has charted

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:56.970
off of those albums, of
course I've heard those.

00:06:56.970 --> 00:07:01.610
In this album, it's a soulfulness to it.

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:04.470
- Yes.
- That, but it has

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:06.983
sort of pop roots bringing soul into it--

00:07:06.983 --> 00:07:08.243
- That soulful part.
- And I'm old enough

00:07:08.243 --> 00:07:11.350
that I'm trying to ask
myself, where would they

00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:15.670
have put this album in
the stovepiped categories

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:16.840
at a record shop?

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:18.600
And I don't think there's a spot.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:20.510
- No, I mean, I guess it's pop

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:21.400
just because it's popular.

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:24.138
- No, no, there's too
much soul, excuse me.

00:07:24.138 --> 00:07:26.593
(Kelly laughs)
No, no no, no, no, no.

00:07:29.016 --> 00:07:30.843
- No, it is a more soulful
pop, well, it's funny though--

00:07:30.843 --> 00:07:32.880
- Because you sing it
and I just have to stop

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:33.713
what I'm doing--
- Thank you!

00:07:33.713 --> 00:07:35.390
- And just feel it.

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:37.762
When it's a Pop song,
you can keep cooking,

00:07:37.762 --> 00:07:41.220
and you shake to it but
you're not moved by it.

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:42.053
- Yeah, thank you.

00:07:42.053 --> 00:07:44.030
- When was the last time
I was moved by a pop song?

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:45.450
- Yeah.
- That's why it's pop.

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:46.840
Because everybody likes it.

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:47.703
- Yeah, it's popular, yeah.

00:07:47.703 --> 00:07:48.910
- It's popular, right.
- Yeah.

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:51.800
- But if you're moved,
that's another kinda--

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:52.780
- That's the record we
wanted to make, yeah.

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:53.613
- Well, you made it.

00:07:53.613 --> 00:07:55.526
- You weaving, bobbing,
weaving, I feel like--

00:07:55.526 --> 00:07:57.544
(both laugh)

00:07:57.544 --> 00:07:59.577
- I'm just saying, you made it.

00:07:59.577 --> 00:08:01.840
- No, it is soulful pop
and it's definitely,

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:03.330
there's definitely a
difference when you listen

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:05.550
to this album versus the other albums.

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:08.519
I mean, one, you can just, I'm happy.

00:08:08.519 --> 00:08:10.810
And it creates a whole different,

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:13.466
that happiness creates
a whole domino effect

00:08:13.466 --> 00:08:18.160
of different sounds and
tones, and even the freedom

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:21.676
that Atlantic has given me
creates a whole different vibe.

00:08:21.676 --> 00:08:24.670
Because an artist in their
form to where they're able

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:27.420
to just go, oh, wait, I
can do whatever I want?

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:28.253
- It's like, what?

00:08:28.253 --> 00:08:30.203
Holy--
- Yeah, it's scary!

00:08:30.203 --> 00:08:31.640
Because you're like,
wait, now it's all on me.

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:32.870
- Now you're accountable.
- Yeah!

00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:34.556
- If this stuff doesn't sell--
- Yeah!

00:08:34.556 --> 00:08:35.389
- Right, right, right.

00:08:35.389 --> 00:08:37.210
- But, luckily, I love, I
was telling my husband this

00:08:37.210 --> 00:08:38.850
even before the album dropped, I was like,

00:08:38.850 --> 00:08:41.320
I've literally been
waiting so long to make

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:44.857
this musical footprint that
like, even if it didn't do

00:08:44.857 --> 00:08:49.857
as well as other things, I
love that I'm still happy

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:50.843
at the end of the day for that.

00:08:50.843 --> 00:08:52.220
Because I've been very blessed.

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:53.940
I've had 15 great years of success,

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:56.100
and even if it were over me, like,

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:59.424
I've had a phenomenal career
and I'm very thankful.

00:08:59.424 --> 00:09:03.660
- So, Brian, from sort of
a vocologist's perspective,

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:05.120
what sets her voice apart?

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:06.940
- Happiness?
- Happiness, I think.

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:07.950
Now that we've heard that.

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:09.250
I love that, though, that's beautiful.

00:09:09.250 --> 00:09:10.723
- And now we know that she was miserable--

00:09:10.723 --> 00:09:12.419
- Before that!
- For the last 15 years

00:09:12.419 --> 00:09:14.157
before this album.

00:09:14.157 --> 00:09:16.791
- But she's what I'd call a Thoroughbred

00:09:16.791 --> 00:09:20.350
of vocalists, in that she can do something

00:09:20.350 --> 00:09:23.300
that's a very high demand on the voice,

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:25.050
the amount of, we call it, connection

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:27.536
at the vocal fold level--
- Vocal fold.

00:09:27.536 --> 00:09:28.369
What is that?

00:09:28.369 --> 00:09:30.550
- Vocal folds are two flaps of tissue

00:09:30.550 --> 00:09:31.845
in the larynx, right here.

00:09:31.845 --> 00:09:33.390
(larynx tapping)
- Oh, don't!

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:35.305
- Oh, wow, I can do that.

00:09:35.305 --> 00:09:38.117
- No, no, I can't, I
don't know what that is!

00:09:38.117 --> 00:09:40.400
You flick your finger into your throat?

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:43.326
- He plucked his throat and made a sound.

00:09:43.326 --> 00:09:44.271
- [Brian] You need good acoustics, right.

00:09:44.271 --> 00:09:46.023
(larynx tapping)

00:09:46.023 --> 00:09:47.209
(larynx tapping)

00:09:47.209 --> 00:09:48.560
(larynx tapping)

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:50.950
- No, I am not doing that
to my neck, I'm sorry!

00:09:50.950 --> 00:09:51.840
- First of all--
- Let's get around this.

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:54.400
- Let me tell you something.
- I think he's a robot.

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:56.820
- I am plucking myself
so hard that it hurts

00:09:56.820 --> 00:09:59.091
and I still can't get
any sound out of it, so.

00:09:59.091 --> 00:10:00.810
- Yeah, you have to close the
vocal folds and you'll get it.

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:02.222
- I close the vocal folds.

00:10:02.222 --> 00:10:03.055
- Like you're getting something, you go.

00:10:03.055 --> 00:10:04.544
(larynx tapping)

00:10:04.544 --> 00:10:05.386
(larynx tapping)

00:10:05.386 --> 00:10:07.181
- Oh!
- Oh, that is!

00:10:07.181 --> 00:10:08.783
- Oh, it worked, nice job!

00:10:08.783 --> 00:10:09.616
- Excuse me, I am not
even attempting that.

00:10:09.616 --> 00:10:10.800
- That's it, that's it, nice.

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:12.307
- Yeah, but you can't breathe.

00:10:12.307 --> 00:10:15.129
- But she's been able to
sustain that kind of production

00:10:15.129 --> 00:10:18.786
really, really well over 15 plus years.

00:10:18.786 --> 00:10:20.930
It's been 15 years in
stardom, but even longer

00:10:20.930 --> 00:10:21.763
than that, so.

00:10:21.763 --> 00:10:23.331
- So, Brain, you performed opera.

00:10:23.331 --> 00:10:24.330
- [Brian] Mhm.

00:10:24.330 --> 00:10:26.070
- Hard rock?
- Yes.

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:27.660
- Funk?
- When I had hair.

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:28.840
- Okay!
- Yes, yes, funk.

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:29.673
- Funk?
- Uh-huh.

00:10:29.673 --> 00:10:30.953
- Not in Indiana.
- No.

00:10:30.953 --> 00:10:32.170
(all laugh)

00:10:32.170 --> 00:10:34.830
I'm guessing, that was
in another music palette.

00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:36.283
- Another life, absolutely.

00:10:36.283 --> 00:10:38.884
- Okay, jazz, even
Indian classical music--

00:10:38.884 --> 00:10:40.008
- Indian classical!
- Which I love.

00:10:40.008 --> 00:10:40.863
I love Indian classical.

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:44.560
Of these genres, not only
the ones you perform,

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:49.560
but that Kelly Clarkson has
shined in, can you rank them

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:52.868
based on the demands on vocal performance?

00:10:52.868 --> 00:10:53.701
- [Chuck] Ooh.

00:10:53.701 --> 00:10:55.760
- Well, I think they all
have different demands,

00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:57.810
especially hard rock,
sort of the gravel people

00:10:57.810 --> 00:10:59.400
expect in the voice, and heavy metal,

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:00.233
that kind of thing.

00:11:00.233 --> 00:11:01.630
- Ooh, gravel, you mean
like a Janice Joplin--

00:11:01.630 --> 00:11:04.683
- Yeah, it's like a
noise, kinda like a snarl.

00:11:04.683 --> 00:11:06.250
(Chuck snarls)
Exactly, yeah.

00:11:06.250 --> 00:11:08.200
- Like you're taking a dump
and drinking Jack Daniels

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:10.311
at the same time!

00:11:10.311 --> 00:11:11.577
♪ Oh Lord ♪

00:11:11.577 --> 00:11:15.544
♪ Won't you buy me a Mercedes Benz ♪

00:11:15.544 --> 00:11:17.866
- Where did you get that--
- Difficult to pass.

00:11:17.866 --> 00:11:18.742
- I can't get that out of my head.

00:11:18.742 --> 00:11:19.575
- [Chuck] It's hard, I'm just saying,

00:11:19.575 --> 00:11:21.710
it's a difficult thing to do.
- You say things I then

00:11:21.710 --> 00:11:23.880
can't get out of my head
for the next 48 hours.

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:25.197
- It's gotta sound more constipated.

00:11:25.197 --> 00:11:26.094
- Right, exactly.

00:11:26.094 --> 00:11:28.017
- Janice, we need more constipation!

00:11:28.017 --> 00:11:28.850
- There it is.

00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:31.495
♪ My friends all have Jaguars ♪

00:11:31.495 --> 00:11:32.958
You know what I mean?

00:11:32.958 --> 00:11:36.730
Yeah, exactly, like, or like, Joe Cocker.

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:37.904
- Yeah, Joe Cocker, man.

00:11:37.904 --> 00:11:39.033
- Yeah, yeah.

00:11:39.033 --> 00:11:41.830
- But then you get to,
like, for Westerners,

00:11:41.830 --> 00:11:44.200
Indian classical music
involves different tonality

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:46.590
and that's really
challenging, so figuring out

00:11:46.590 --> 00:11:47.423
how to do that.

00:11:47.423 --> 00:11:49.340
Luckily I was not the lead singer,

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:52.670
I was the head of a
backing group behind it.

00:11:52.670 --> 00:11:53.940
- I'm Sorry, I don't know anything

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:55.070
about Indian classical music.

00:11:55.070 --> 00:11:57.270
Is that the music that goes
on for like a half hour

00:11:57.270 --> 00:11:58.160
without any change?

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:00.230
- It can go on a long
time with just a beat.

00:12:00.230 --> 00:12:01.580
- Yeah.
- Yeah, okay cool.

00:12:02.431 --> 00:12:03.264
- And they kinda chant.

00:12:03.264 --> 00:12:04.097
- And sitar's in there somewhere, too.

00:12:04.097 --> 00:12:05.150
- Yeah.
- Okay, cool, cool.

00:12:05.150 --> 00:12:08.020
- Yeah.
- So, in these genres, then,

00:12:08.020 --> 00:12:13.020
because they come from such
different cultural places,

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:16.430
country, Indian, funk, pop,

00:12:16.430 --> 00:12:21.430
is it technique or do you
have to actually feel it?

00:12:21.770 --> 00:12:26.770
Do you need some genetic
link back into that place

00:12:27.050 --> 00:12:30.850
to draw from, in order
to perform or to excel?

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:31.750
- Both.

00:12:31.750 --> 00:12:33.240
I think there's a technique to it,

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:35.876
there's a way you use
your voice that you can

00:12:35.876 --> 00:12:38.500
actually define in a scientific way,

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:39.820
what the people are doing.

00:12:39.820 --> 00:12:41.550
It's no longer as much of a mastery.

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:44.060
But then there's also
an exposure to a genre

00:12:44.060 --> 00:12:47.695
that's absolutely imperative
someone have, or else--

00:12:47.695 --> 00:12:48.598
- [Neil] It's a cultural thing.

00:12:48.598 --> 00:12:49.431
- They sound like a poser.

00:12:49.431 --> 00:12:51.390
Yeah, you have to grow up
loving it, and it's yours.

00:12:51.390 --> 00:12:53.855
- [Neil] And it's gotta
be under your skin.

00:12:53.855 --> 00:12:54.688
- Yeah.

00:12:54.688 --> 00:12:55.840
I have people who come
to me as opera singers,

00:12:55.840 --> 00:12:58.630
and then they're like, I
wanna do a little more rock.

00:12:58.630 --> 00:13:00.543
And I'm like, do you like rock?

00:13:00.543 --> 00:13:01.378
And they're like, not really.

00:13:01.378 --> 00:13:02.540
And so--
- Then get the hell out.

00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:04.024
- Yeah, they're gonna sound funny.

00:13:04.024 --> 00:13:04.857
- Go on down the hallway.

00:13:04.857 --> 00:13:09.410
So, I asked Kelly about
how she conveys feelings

00:13:09.410 --> 00:13:11.710
and emotions in her singing,
so let's check it out.

00:13:11.710 --> 00:13:15.222
That's got me thinking, I
can read a poem on a page,

00:13:15.222 --> 00:13:20.222
a rhyme, and it'll mean
something, informationally,

00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:23.893
to me perhaps, but if I hear you sing it

00:13:23.893 --> 00:13:28.893
there's a whole other conduit
of communication going on.

00:13:29.020 --> 00:13:30.200
- Oh, definitely, it's emotions.

00:13:30.200 --> 00:13:34.147
- And, are you, you must
be self-aware of that,

00:13:34.147 --> 00:13:36.964
and you can go in there
and you can manipulate it--

00:13:36.964 --> 00:13:38.681
- Yeah, I mean, that's--

00:13:38.681 --> 00:13:41.703
- You, how do you grok that?

00:13:42.886 --> 00:13:45.950
- I think it's, I don't
even think about it.

00:13:45.950 --> 00:13:49.170
I think I'm just so, I've
always been touched by music

00:13:49.170 --> 00:13:50.440
even before I knew I could sing.

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:53.670
I loved dancing, I loved
music, it was a safe place

00:13:53.670 --> 00:13:57.560
for me, it was like a, it
was a whole different kind

00:13:57.560 --> 00:14:01.185
of learning, just with
people's lives, whether it was

00:14:01.185 --> 00:14:05.220
rock, rap, pop, like,
country, whatever you're

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:08.530
listening to, the music
is a very good indication

00:14:08.530 --> 00:14:11.770
of the time, in that timeline
that you're referring to.

00:14:11.770 --> 00:14:14.090
Music, if you look at
it, it always follows it

00:14:14.090 --> 00:14:17.270
because obviously artists
are affected by our nature

00:14:17.270 --> 00:14:20.070
and nurture, a bit, so yeah, I dunno--

00:14:20.070 --> 00:14:20.903
- But what's your goal?

00:14:20.903 --> 00:14:22.545
You know you can reach people emotionally,

00:14:22.545 --> 00:14:25.550
but you also would have
the capacity in a song

00:14:25.550 --> 00:14:29.450
because a song is a story, to
reach people informationally.

00:14:29.450 --> 00:14:30.283
- Yeah, I mean, you wanna--

00:14:30.283 --> 00:14:31.116
- So what's your balance?

00:14:31.116 --> 00:14:32.155
What's your balance?

00:14:32.155 --> 00:14:35.410
- I like to be a bit
poetic but at the same time

00:14:35.410 --> 00:14:38.258
keep the story simple
enough to where people

00:14:38.258 --> 00:14:42.160
aren't focused on the words of the story,

00:14:42.160 --> 00:14:44.150
it's more focused on the
feelings of the story.

00:14:44.150 --> 00:14:47.660
It's more focused on
how that made me feel,

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:49.860
and how that, because I'm
more of an emotional person.

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:51.470
- Because I'd just go
out and read a damn book

00:14:51.470 --> 00:14:52.303
if I want words.
- Yeah.

00:14:52.303 --> 00:14:54.063
- Too many words.
- Yeah.

00:14:54.063 --> 00:14:56.540
And it's, I dunno, I've
always been the one

00:14:56.540 --> 00:14:58.910
to focus more on, like,
I always feel like, okay.

00:14:58.910 --> 00:15:01.240
I know I talk about Meryl
Streep a lot but my point is--

00:15:01.240 --> 00:15:02.230
- Not yet, on this show.

00:15:02.230 --> 00:15:03.063
- I know, but I'm just saying.

00:15:03.063 --> 00:15:03.896
I do talk about her a lot.

00:15:03.896 --> 00:15:06.187
But here's my point, I've
never said this before.

00:15:06.187 --> 00:15:07.960
- And I love me some Meryl Streep.

00:15:07.960 --> 00:15:11.350
- Oh, but I feel like
she's one of those people,

00:15:11.350 --> 00:15:13.360
it could be a silent movie

00:15:13.360 --> 00:15:17.860
and she would effortlessly,
flawlessly dictate

00:15:17.860 --> 00:15:22.200
by her emotions, her eyes,
just her, every part of her,

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:24.010
you would know exactly what she's saying

00:15:24.010 --> 00:15:25.940
without her saying anything
through the entire movie.

00:15:25.940 --> 00:15:28.470
And I think she would of
been the most fantastic

00:15:28.470 --> 00:15:31.550
silent actress as well,
because she's got that art

00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:33.450
to her whole, like, physical body,

00:15:33.450 --> 00:15:36.330
her emotional state, like,
her eyes, and her mouth,

00:15:36.330 --> 00:15:38.900
and her, all of that,
whether it's Miranda Priestly

00:15:38.900 --> 00:15:41.513
or whatever character she's playing--

00:15:41.513 --> 00:15:43.250
- From The Devil Wears Prada,
for those not in the know.

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:45.010
- Or if it's Out of Africa
or something, but whatever

00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:47.930
those are, she does so much
and I think that as a singer,

00:15:47.930 --> 00:15:51.470
too, that's what I try
to do, like, with songs.

00:15:51.470 --> 00:15:55.360
I try to like, whatever the words are,

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:56.570
I could be not singing words at all,

00:15:56.570 --> 00:15:59.330
but just melody, and by my intensity

00:15:59.330 --> 00:16:01.144
or by--
- The modulation of that?

00:16:01.144 --> 00:16:04.360
- My emotion, yeah, you
could almost tell the story

00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:05.900
I'm telling without there even been words.

00:16:05.900 --> 00:16:08.250
- Yeah, so Brian, is there, would you say

00:16:08.250 --> 00:16:11.943
there's an evolutionary
propensity to respond

00:16:11.943 --> 00:16:14.670
to a singing voice as opposed

00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:16.780
to just a spoken voice, and why?

00:16:16.780 --> 00:16:19.550
I mean, if you're just
communicating information,

00:16:19.550 --> 00:16:21.650
I'll just utter a sentence.

00:16:21.650 --> 00:16:23.090
But somehow that doesn't work as well

00:16:23.090 --> 00:16:24.390
as if I sing a sentence.

00:16:24.390 --> 00:16:27.670
- Yeah, I think singing
in the different timbres

00:16:27.670 --> 00:16:30.170
that you have and ranges
that you're singing

00:16:30.170 --> 00:16:32.600
are more impactful with communication.

00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:35.430
Darwin had a thought that in hominids,

00:16:35.430 --> 00:16:40.430
like, early progenitors of
man, that the first utterances

00:16:40.770 --> 00:16:42.253
were probably musical.

00:16:43.141 --> 00:16:45.347
So I mean, if you think
about most species--

00:16:45.347 --> 00:16:47.155
- Like, doo-wop, or?
- Yeah, like doo-wop.

00:16:47.155 --> 00:16:48.371
(Neil laughs)

00:16:48.371 --> 00:16:49.926
Bunch of cavemen just like--

00:16:49.926 --> 00:16:51.701
♪ Do do, do do do ♪

00:16:51.701 --> 00:16:52.534
♪ Do do dah ♪

00:16:52.534 --> 00:16:53.792
♪ We don't have vocabulary yet ♪

00:16:53.792 --> 00:16:54.731
♪ So it's just ♪

00:16:54.731 --> 00:16:56.580
♪ Bap a do bop a do bop ♪

00:16:56.580 --> 00:16:57.607
- They can't say you're out of tune.

00:16:57.607 --> 00:16:58.693
- That's when scat was invented.

00:16:58.693 --> 00:17:00.387
- Yeah, scat.

00:17:00.387 --> 00:17:03.760
- But most species have
some kind of song for mating

00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:07.431
and attracting people
and identifying family

00:17:07.431 --> 00:17:09.530
and territory and all those things.

00:17:09.530 --> 00:17:12.380
- I'm just seeing an early hominid

00:17:12.380 --> 00:17:15.589
standing outside of a cave going.

00:17:15.589 --> 00:17:17.856
♪ My mind's telling me yes ♪

00:17:17.856 --> 00:17:19.341
♪ But my body ♪

00:17:19.341 --> 00:17:22.510
♪ My body's telling me no ♪

00:17:22.510 --> 00:17:23.683
Come on out here, girl.

00:17:24.937 --> 00:17:26.270
♪ Ah ♪

00:17:26.270 --> 00:17:28.053
- Oh, Lord.
- Sorry.

00:17:29.222 --> 00:17:30.055
Go ahead.

00:17:30.055 --> 00:17:31.160
- Well.
- I'm sorry.

00:17:31.160 --> 00:17:35.580
- I had to ask Kelly about the basis for,

00:17:35.580 --> 00:17:38.380
the thematic basis for her current album.

00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:39.280
So let's check it out.

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:41.180
The Meaning of Life.

00:17:41.180 --> 00:17:44.258
- When you say it like that
it's far more dramatic.

00:17:44.258 --> 00:17:45.091
(both laugh)

00:17:45.091 --> 00:17:48.580
- That's kinda bodacious
audacious to be calling

00:17:48.580 --> 00:17:49.590
your album--
- It was bold.

00:17:49.590 --> 00:17:50.630
- It was bold.

00:17:50.630 --> 00:17:52.090
- You can figure out the meaning of life

00:17:52.090 --> 00:17:53.401
when you buy my album.

00:17:53.401 --> 00:17:55.142
(both laugh)

00:17:55.142 --> 00:17:55.975
That's it.

00:17:55.975 --> 00:17:56.808
- That's a great selling point!

00:17:56.808 --> 00:17:58.226
- It's like Charlie and
the Chocolate Factory.

00:17:58.226 --> 00:17:59.960
The golden ticket.

00:17:59.960 --> 00:18:02.402
- Wow, okay, so that means
I don't get to learn it

00:18:02.402 --> 00:18:03.446
here and now--

00:18:03.446 --> 00:18:04.490
- Yeah, not just yet,
not until you get it.

00:18:04.490 --> 00:18:05.890
I'm just sayin'.
- Ooh.

00:18:05.890 --> 00:18:07.850
So, the meaning of life.

00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:10.710
Is this something you've
known for a while,

00:18:10.710 --> 00:18:11.593
or just kinda came onto it?

00:18:11.593 --> 00:18:14.100
- No, I mean, I think we're
all under construction,

00:18:14.100 --> 00:18:16.467
and I think the point of me doing it--

00:18:16.467 --> 00:18:17.700
- I like that, we're
all under construction.

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:18.970
- We are!
- Beautiful.

00:18:18.970 --> 00:18:20.665
- Yeah, and I think the point of it--

00:18:20.665 --> 00:18:22.427
- In fact, I think the
people who are fully built,

00:18:22.427 --> 00:18:24.820
they're no longer learning anything.

00:18:24.820 --> 00:18:27.870
- Well, and that's, first of
all, any person that thinks

00:18:27.870 --> 00:18:31.410
they've got it figured out
completely is a total dork, like.

00:18:31.410 --> 00:18:33.160
- It means they really
haven't figured it out

00:18:33.160 --> 00:18:34.029
because they haven't figured it out

00:18:34.029 --> 00:18:34.980
that they haven't figured it out.

00:18:34.980 --> 00:18:37.198
- Yeah, what if you're a philosopher?

00:18:37.198 --> 00:18:38.193
Yes.

00:18:39.151 --> 00:18:42.570
But I do think that
Meaning of Life is really

00:18:42.570 --> 00:18:45.090
just about connection, and making sure

00:18:45.090 --> 00:18:46.870
they're positive connections.

00:18:46.870 --> 00:18:47.906
There's always people--
- People to people.

00:18:47.906 --> 00:18:49.190
- Yeah.
- But how about

00:18:49.190 --> 00:18:51.050
all the negative people who are out there?

00:18:51.050 --> 00:18:53.839
- You just push them outside your bubble.

00:18:53.839 --> 00:18:55.080
(both laugh)
- Yeah.

00:18:55.080 --> 00:18:55.913
And you know what?

00:18:55.913 --> 00:18:57.840
I have a tattoo for that.

00:18:57.840 --> 00:18:59.760
A pastor told me--
- Most people have an app

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:00.593
for that, you have a tattoo.

00:19:00.593 --> 00:19:02.000
- I know, I have a tattoo,
because I really needed

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:03.203
the daily remember.

00:19:03.203 --> 00:19:06.430
I'm working in an industry
where people can be

00:19:06.430 --> 00:19:09.640
not-so-lovely, and so I
have a tattoo and my pastor

00:19:09.640 --> 00:19:11.210
told me this when I was
a kid, and I loved him.

00:19:11.210 --> 00:19:13.370
He was like, one of those
people that you wanted,

00:19:13.370 --> 00:19:15.680
you're like, what do
you have in your life,

00:19:15.680 --> 00:19:16.780
because I wanna be like you.

00:19:16.780 --> 00:19:20.010
He just had a light about
him, and I just thought

00:19:20.010 --> 00:19:24.100
he was special, and he said
one time that there's gonna

00:19:24.100 --> 00:19:25.780
be people in your life
and it's easy to love

00:19:25.780 --> 00:19:27.160
the lovely people, but it's not easy

00:19:27.160 --> 00:19:28.440
to love the unlovely.

00:19:28.440 --> 00:19:31.501
And I thought that was really
poignant, and I thought,

00:19:31.501 --> 00:19:34.920
you have to love the
people that are unlovely

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:37.070
even more, because they
weren't loved enough, so.

00:19:37.070 --> 00:19:39.390
- Mm, they're missing something in life.

00:19:39.390 --> 00:19:41.270
- Yes, so, I dunno.

00:19:41.270 --> 00:19:43.940
I think it's about just trying to make

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:47.500
those positive connections and
really, I think it's simple.

00:19:47.500 --> 00:19:49.010
I think we complicate it as humans,

00:19:49.010 --> 00:19:50.250
because sometimes we try to figure out

00:19:50.250 --> 00:19:51.787
and sometimes you can't.

00:19:53.240 --> 00:19:55.107
- But you're--
- Well, sometimes you can.

00:19:55.107 --> 00:19:56.610
I can't.

00:19:56.610 --> 00:19:58.080
- Your mother was a schoolteacher.

00:19:58.080 --> 00:19:58.913
- She was.

00:19:58.913 --> 00:20:01.510
- So, here you are making it
part of your life's mission

00:20:01.510 --> 00:20:03.990
to figure things out, and a schoolteacher,

00:20:03.990 --> 00:20:06.510
they're trying to get their
kids to figure stuff out.

00:20:06.510 --> 00:20:07.343
- Yeah, the teachers question.

00:20:07.343 --> 00:20:09.120
- So, how much of that touched you?

00:20:09.120 --> 00:20:11.341
- I love, I mean, she always
taught me to question,

00:20:11.341 --> 00:20:13.605
and I think that's the best part of,

00:20:13.605 --> 00:20:16.420
whether you're talking
about faith, politics,

00:20:16.420 --> 00:20:20.610
life, any, love, it's
always to question it.

00:20:20.610 --> 00:20:22.220
Like, I love that.

00:20:22.220 --> 00:20:24.610
I love to question whatever's happening

00:20:24.610 --> 00:20:27.039
in the moment, one to make sure it's real,

00:20:27.039 --> 00:20:32.039
and two, just because I don't think,

00:20:32.060 --> 00:20:33.900
when you stop questioning,
you stop asking,

00:20:33.900 --> 00:20:36.200
you stop caring, right?

00:20:36.200 --> 00:20:37.179
I dunno.

00:20:37.179 --> 00:20:38.750
- I can't say it better than that.

00:20:38.750 --> 00:20:39.583
- I bet you can!

00:20:40.457 --> 00:20:43.430
I bet you can effortlessly!

00:20:43.430 --> 00:20:46.260
- Coming up on the science
of voice and my interview

00:20:46.260 --> 00:20:49.633
with Kelly Clarkson,
when StarTalk returns.

00:20:52.270 --> 00:20:54.190
Welcome back to StarTalk.

00:20:54.190 --> 00:20:56.253
I'm with my co-host, Chuck Nice.

00:20:56.253 --> 00:20:57.360
- That's right.
- And our expert guest

00:20:57.360 --> 00:21:00.398
in this episode, Brian
Gill, professor of voice

00:21:00.398 --> 00:21:03.280
at Indiana University.
- Hello.

00:21:03.280 --> 00:21:04.610
- Bloomington.
- Yeah.

00:21:04.610 --> 00:21:06.160
- Oh, yeah.

00:21:06.160 --> 00:21:08.330
We're talking about the
science of the singing voice,

00:21:08.330 --> 00:21:10.840
featuring my interview
with Kelly Clarkson.

00:21:10.840 --> 00:21:12.750
- Yeah.
- And I just had something

00:21:12.750 --> 00:21:13.583
to follow up.

00:21:13.583 --> 00:21:16.910
We talked about your
capacity to bring an emotion

00:21:16.910 --> 00:21:20.295
or an authenticity to
what it is you're saying,

00:21:20.295 --> 00:21:25.050
and I remembered, I liked
James Taylor, but not enough

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:26.010
to buy all his albums.

00:21:26.010 --> 00:21:27.280
I waited 'till he had his Greatest Hits

00:21:27.280 --> 00:21:28.680
and I bought the Greatest Hits album.

00:21:28.680 --> 00:21:30.310
- Mm, okay.
- And on there--

00:21:30.310 --> 00:21:31.840
- Why, were you having trouble sleeping?

00:21:31.840 --> 00:21:33.499
(Neil laughs)

00:21:33.499 --> 00:21:34.332
- Stop it!

00:21:35.500 --> 00:21:38.030
My sister can't get enough of him, so,

00:21:38.030 --> 00:21:39.950
goes to all his concerts and everything.

00:21:39.950 --> 00:21:43.440
And so I'm listening to
the album and I'm a fan

00:21:43.440 --> 00:21:46.602
of the blues, and in there he has--

00:21:46.602 --> 00:21:49.240
- A blues song?
- The Steamroller Blues.

00:21:49.240 --> 00:21:51.880
- James Taylor?
- He has a blues song.

00:21:51.880 --> 00:21:54.183
And then I said, okay, he's trying,

00:21:55.818 --> 00:22:00.420
but he went to a prep
school in Massachusets

00:22:00.420 --> 00:22:03.070
in high school, and goes
to Martha's Vineyard,

00:22:03.070 --> 00:22:06.460
and how many blues singers
ever came outta Massachusets?

00:22:06.460 --> 00:22:09.478
And so I couldn't--

00:22:09.478 --> 00:22:11.543
♪ Woke up this morning ♪

00:22:11.543 --> 00:22:14.379
♪ But the Volvo wouldn't start ♪

00:22:14.379 --> 00:22:16.310
♪ Dun duh nuh nuh nuh ♪

00:22:16.310 --> 00:22:18.210
- He does drop the f-bomb,
though, in that one.

00:22:18.210 --> 00:22:19.043
- He does!

00:22:19.043 --> 00:22:19.876
- So I think that does--

00:22:19.876 --> 00:22:20.930
- Is that what that would--

00:22:20.930 --> 00:22:22.530
- Yeah, it's improv with him.

00:22:22.530 --> 00:22:24.680
- It just wasn't working for me.

00:22:24.680 --> 00:22:27.075
He was not convincing as just,

00:22:27.075 --> 00:22:28.991
it wasn't working.

00:22:28.991 --> 00:22:31.400
- It wasn't working for him
either, I'm telling you.

00:22:31.400 --> 00:22:32.623
I'm letting you know.

00:22:34.140 --> 00:22:37.630
But I think the blues as a
genre is probably the one

00:22:37.630 --> 00:22:40.174
that you would have to feel the most,

00:22:40.174 --> 00:22:41.290
in order to--
- I'm thinking, personally.

00:22:41.290 --> 00:22:43.238
I tweeted recently the list of states

00:22:43.238 --> 00:22:45.889
that blues singers
don't tend to come from.

00:22:45.889 --> 00:22:47.309
(all laugh)

00:22:47.309 --> 00:22:48.190
- Oh, I love that!

00:22:48.190 --> 00:22:50.630
Utah on top of the list?
- Utah!

00:22:50.630 --> 00:22:53.210
- Oh my God.
- New Hampshire.

00:22:53.210 --> 00:22:55.390
- Yes, that's amazing!

00:22:55.390 --> 00:22:56.470
How did I miss that tweet?

00:22:56.470 --> 00:22:57.950
That's amazing!

00:22:57.950 --> 00:22:58.783
- How did you miss that?

00:22:58.783 --> 00:22:59.616
- How did I miss that tweet?

00:22:59.616 --> 00:23:00.449
- I'm just saying.
- That is phenomenal.

00:23:00.449 --> 00:23:03.703
- Yeah, you got be from, like,
from Alabama, Mississippi.

00:23:04.757 --> 00:23:06.290
You gotta be po' in Chicago.

00:23:06.290 --> 00:23:07.980
- You gotta be po' in Chicago.

00:23:07.980 --> 00:23:10.310
That's it, right, Louisiana, right.

00:23:10.310 --> 00:23:11.930
- At some price.
- You need something right.

00:23:11.930 --> 00:23:13.140
Exactly.

00:23:13.140 --> 00:23:15.400
There's got to be some poor black people

00:23:15.400 --> 00:23:18.880
near you somewhere, that's what it is!

00:23:18.880 --> 00:23:20.384
- Or you don't have the blues.

00:23:20.384 --> 00:23:21.940
- Oh, you're from Colorado, no?

00:23:21.940 --> 00:23:24.343
- There's no Colorado
blues, you know what I mean?

00:23:24.343 --> 00:23:25.964
What do they sing about?

00:23:25.964 --> 00:23:28.470
- Rocky Mountain high.

00:23:28.470 --> 00:23:29.442
- Yeah, exactly.

00:23:29.442 --> 00:23:31.826
♪ My skis broke ♪

00:23:31.826 --> 00:23:33.338
♪ Duh nuh nuh nuh nuh ♪

00:23:33.338 --> 00:23:34.770
- Yeah, exactly, you know what I mean?

00:23:34.770 --> 00:23:36.646
No hot chocolate at the lodge?

00:23:36.646 --> 00:23:38.047
Like, really?

00:23:38.047 --> 00:23:40.975
- Only three marshmallows?
- Okay, yeah.

00:23:40.975 --> 00:23:42.640
Dude, that's brilliant.

00:23:42.640 --> 00:23:45.520
- I'm just saying, you've
gotta find that tweet.

00:23:45.520 --> 00:23:47.460
It's about six states.

00:23:47.460 --> 00:23:48.620
- Yeah, that is brilliant.

00:23:48.620 --> 00:23:51.240
And the Utah blues, I'm
going home to write that.

00:23:51.240 --> 00:23:54.230
I'm going to write that song
tonight, I guarantee you.

00:23:54.230 --> 00:23:56.060
The Utah blues.

00:23:56.060 --> 00:23:57.310
I can't wait.

00:23:57.310 --> 00:23:59.970
- So, let's get back to my
interview with Kelly Clarkson

00:23:59.970 --> 00:24:01.640
and see where that takes us next.

00:24:01.640 --> 00:24:02.473
Check it out.

00:24:02.473 --> 00:24:05.930
Do you think of your voice
as an instrument that needs

00:24:05.930 --> 00:24:07.620
cultivating and care and--

00:24:07.620 --> 00:24:09.210
- Yes.
- Okay.

00:24:09.210 --> 00:24:10.520
- Yeah, no, I have to,

00:24:10.520 --> 00:24:11.760
I mean,
- The reason why I asked

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:14.511
is because we were in the
green room before you performed

00:24:14.511 --> 00:24:19.511
in the YouTube space, and
I love to call it space.

00:24:19.560 --> 00:24:21.930
- Yeah, you would.
- YouTube space, yes.

00:24:21.930 --> 00:24:24.216
If you got space in your
name, I'm good with it!

00:24:24.216 --> 00:24:27.410
But before you were just
yucking it up with everybody

00:24:27.410 --> 00:24:29.050
in the green room, and then
you just walk out there

00:24:29.050 --> 00:24:33.663
and then out comes this, how do you, how--

00:24:34.530 --> 00:24:36.140
- Well, I warmed up earlier this morning,

00:24:36.140 --> 00:24:40.100
so I worked before, I've been
working since this morning.

00:24:40.100 --> 00:24:44.160
So, I definitely aim to warm up every day.

00:24:44.160 --> 00:24:46.330
It's just like a speaker,
like a public speaker,

00:24:46.330 --> 00:24:48.760
like, your voice is, or a runner.

00:24:48.760 --> 00:24:50.483
Your voice is a muscle,
it's these little things

00:24:50.483 --> 00:24:52.654
that you have to keep trained.

00:24:52.654 --> 00:24:55.080
I know some singers don't have to.

00:24:55.080 --> 00:24:57.610
I think I heard Jennifer, in an interview,

00:24:57.610 --> 00:24:59.367
Jennifer Hudson, she said
she didn't, I'm sorry.

00:24:59.367 --> 00:25:00.590
- All you first name people.

00:25:00.590 --> 00:25:01.423
- I'm sorry!

00:25:01.423 --> 00:25:02.997
- I'm gonna start
namedropping first names.

00:25:02.997 --> 00:25:03.913
- No, no, no!
- Okay.

00:25:03.913 --> 00:25:05.839
- I say it because--
- Me and Steven.

00:25:05.839 --> 00:25:06.672
Steven Hawking, okay, yeah.

00:25:06.672 --> 00:25:10.090
- Okay.
- Alright, my turn.

00:25:10.090 --> 00:25:13.420
My turn, okay.
- My turn.

00:25:13.420 --> 00:25:16.299
Yeah, you're in a
different pool of people.

00:25:16.299 --> 00:25:17.722
- I was meeting Ike the other day.

00:25:17.722 --> 00:25:19.133
Like, Isaac Newton, yes.

00:25:20.882 --> 00:25:23.069
Jennifer Hudson.
- No, I'm saying she's

00:25:23.069 --> 00:25:24.510
a huge, big vocalist.

00:25:24.510 --> 00:25:25.880
- Another American Idol person.

00:25:25.880 --> 00:25:26.780
- As well, yes.

00:25:26.780 --> 00:25:29.290
And she's a big vocalist and she doesn't.

00:25:29.290 --> 00:25:31.060
She said in interviews,
and I found it shocking.

00:25:31.060 --> 00:25:33.160
She says, "I don't warm up
at all, it just happens."

00:25:33.160 --> 00:25:34.786
So, I'm not like that.

00:25:34.786 --> 00:25:37.080
I have to warm up a bit.

00:25:37.080 --> 00:25:39.649
I can do it, it's just
not gonna sound as good.

00:25:39.649 --> 00:25:43.210
- Okay, not to call you out,
but you are 15 years older

00:25:43.210 --> 00:25:45.843
than you were when you won American Idol.

00:25:45.843 --> 00:25:46.676
- Yes.

00:25:46.676 --> 00:25:50.490
- So, can you, is the
warm-up the same now?

00:25:50.490 --> 00:25:52.370
- Oh, it's easier.
- Easier!

00:25:52.370 --> 00:25:53.480
- Yeah, I think.
- Why?

00:25:53.480 --> 00:25:56.100
- It's funny, I saw Tony Bennett at this--

00:25:56.100 --> 00:25:58.340
- Oh, good Tony Bennet, thank
you, no, no, that's fine.

00:25:58.340 --> 00:25:59.340
Have both names.

00:25:59.340 --> 00:26:00.510
Yeah, I saw Tony.

00:26:00.510 --> 00:26:02.320
- No, I'm saying I--
- We're tight.

00:26:02.320 --> 00:26:05.050
- I was at this event
and this was years ago,

00:26:05.050 --> 00:26:06.907
and he was saying, and
he was 80-something,

00:26:06.907 --> 00:26:10.110
and I was like, I mean,
he killed all of us.

00:26:10.110 --> 00:26:12.950
He was amazing.

00:26:12.950 --> 00:26:15.680
And I'm a Tony Bennett
fan, but I didn't know.

00:26:15.680 --> 00:26:17.480
I thought maybe the older
we get, I don't know,

00:26:17.480 --> 00:26:18.540
sometimes people sound different.

00:26:18.540 --> 00:26:21.510
He sounds even better,
like a fine glass of wine.

00:26:21.510 --> 00:26:22.490
It's so beautiful.

00:26:22.490 --> 00:26:27.110
And I will say if you're
doing things correctly

00:26:27.110 --> 00:26:28.770
and you're taking care of yourself

00:26:28.770 --> 00:26:30.359
and you're not ruining your chords,

00:26:30.359 --> 00:26:32.177
you're not ruining your
chords, not getting,

00:26:32.177 --> 00:26:35.166
you're not doing things you're
not supposed to be doing.

00:26:35.166 --> 00:26:36.360
- Your vocal chords or
your musical chords?

00:26:36.360 --> 00:26:37.193
- Your vocal chords.

00:26:37.193 --> 00:26:39.553
- Is that a weird question?
- I mean if you're--

00:26:39.553 --> 00:26:41.260
I dunno.
- No, I mean

00:26:41.260 --> 00:26:42.940
my vocal chords, like, you're not,

00:26:42.940 --> 00:26:45.070
you hear of people having
surgery and all that stuff.

00:26:45.070 --> 00:26:47.380
If you're taking care of yourself I think

00:26:47.380 --> 00:26:48.930
you get better with age.

00:26:48.930 --> 00:26:51.820
Your voice sounds more lived
in and it sounds, like,

00:26:51.820 --> 00:26:53.180
I can go lower, I can go higher.

00:26:53.180 --> 00:26:54.500
- I like that phrased, lived in.

00:26:54.500 --> 00:26:55.333
- Yeah.

00:26:55.333 --> 00:26:56.560
- Like a good pair of boots.

00:26:56.560 --> 00:26:57.810
Just to be clear, in the green room

00:26:57.810 --> 00:27:01.000
before she went out, I
understated what she was doing

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:01.833
in the green room.

00:27:01.833 --> 00:27:04.620
She had friends, her husband, her agent,

00:27:04.620 --> 00:27:07.140
her booking, everybody, and
she's just yucking it up

00:27:07.140 --> 00:27:09.991
with everybody while she's
eating chicken and waffles.

00:27:09.991 --> 00:27:11.408
- Wow.

00:27:11.408 --> 00:27:13.660
- Just scarfing down chicken and waffles.

00:27:13.660 --> 00:27:15.950
- I'm thinking--
- That's a good warmup.

00:27:15.950 --> 00:27:18.633
- Is she about, what's
about to happen after this?

00:27:18.633 --> 00:27:19.710
I had no idea.

00:27:19.710 --> 00:27:21.619
She went out there--
- And ripped it apart.

00:27:21.619 --> 00:27:23.874
- And ripped us, just tore a new one,

00:27:23.874 --> 00:27:25.890
and oh, my.

00:27:25.890 --> 00:27:29.810
So tell me about the
anatomy of the vocal chords.

00:27:29.810 --> 00:27:31.467
- So, well, there are two--

00:27:31.467 --> 00:27:33.350
- And while you're at it,
I'd like to distinguish them,

00:27:33.350 --> 00:27:36.920
if you can, from that of other
primates or anything else

00:27:36.920 --> 00:27:40.000
that would make sounds
like birds or whales.

00:27:40.000 --> 00:27:42.220
- One of the main things in human beings,

00:27:42.220 --> 00:27:44.740
the larynx, which is the
voice box, right here,

00:27:44.740 --> 00:27:48.640
has descended, and so it
creates a bent resonator

00:27:48.640 --> 00:27:50.430
and that's like a space
here and then a space

00:27:50.430 --> 00:27:51.300
going this way.

00:27:51.300 --> 00:27:52.952
They're roughly about the same size.

00:27:52.952 --> 00:27:53.785
- [Neil] So upwards, and then forwards.

00:27:53.785 --> 00:27:55.980
- Yeah, all the other
species have a larynx that's

00:27:55.980 --> 00:27:58.880
way up there, and so they
only use it for sounds

00:27:58.880 --> 00:28:00.550
and they don't use their tongue very much

00:28:00.550 --> 00:28:02.610
for articulation when
they're making sounds.

00:28:02.610 --> 00:28:05.120
So, that's unique about human beings.

00:28:05.120 --> 00:28:08.470
- So, the other sounds--
- Here it comes.

00:28:08.470 --> 00:28:11.421
- Like the Nutty Professor, he was like.

00:28:11.421 --> 00:28:14.088
(Chuck babbles)

00:28:15.410 --> 00:28:16.243
So, anyway.

00:28:17.315 --> 00:28:19.300
- So, that's one of the
unique features, and then

00:28:19.300 --> 00:28:22.510
you've got, with voice
you've got this power source

00:28:22.510 --> 00:28:23.970
which is the air that
comes from your lungs,

00:28:23.970 --> 00:28:26.120
you've got the tissue
here, the vocal folds

00:28:26.120 --> 00:28:28.520
that vibrate, that create
sounds, and then that sound

00:28:28.520 --> 00:28:30.260
is filtered through the space above it.

00:28:30.260 --> 00:28:33.656
So that's the basics of the
way the instrument works.

00:28:33.656 --> 00:28:36.370
- Does that serve any purpose for us

00:28:36.370 --> 00:28:39.640
socially or otherwise that
we would be so different?

00:28:39.640 --> 00:28:42.310
- Yeah, yeah, and the way
we use our voice, too,

00:28:42.310 --> 00:28:43.560
serves a purpose.

00:28:43.560 --> 00:28:45.310
There was a study not
too long ago where people

00:28:45.310 --> 00:28:49.876
could judge trustworthiness and dominance

00:28:49.876 --> 00:28:54.410
in I think it was 500 milliseconds
of listening to someone.

00:28:54.410 --> 00:28:55.574
- Really?
- Yes.

00:28:55.574 --> 00:28:56.640
- Oh, so--
- Right away.

00:28:56.640 --> 00:28:59.820
- Deep information
about social interaction

00:28:59.820 --> 00:29:01.913
is communicated just by
the tonality of your voice.

00:29:01.913 --> 00:29:03.610
- [Brian] Just 500 milliseconds.

00:29:03.610 --> 00:29:05.730
- Wow.
- And so there's certain

00:29:05.730 --> 00:29:08.780
habits that people have,
that are often rated higher,

00:29:08.780 --> 00:29:13.000
or the way a woman modulates
the voice by choosing

00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:14.796
different pitches and things like that.

00:29:14.796 --> 00:29:16.875
That's trustworthy.

00:29:16.875 --> 00:29:17.905
A person who doesn't do that,

00:29:17.905 --> 00:29:18.738
and then the same thing with male--

00:29:18.738 --> 00:29:19.835
- [Neil] If they're valley girl?

