00:31:18.892 --> 00:31:19.725
- I like that HBO series, I'm sorry.

00:31:19.725 --> 00:31:21.710
Shoutout to Mirella Freni, though.

00:31:21.710 --> 00:31:23.220
She's one of the best in the opera world

00:31:23.220 --> 00:31:26.330
and she's 80 something now
and still sounds beautiful.

00:31:26.330 --> 00:31:28.242
- At 80?
- At 80 something, yeah.

00:31:28.242 --> 00:31:29.075
- So, we send them out to pasture.

00:31:29.075 --> 00:31:29.930
So, what's going on there?

00:31:29.930 --> 00:31:31.550
- Yeah, so what happens,
there's a breakdown

00:31:31.550 --> 00:31:32.950
in the voice, and one of
the things that jumped out

00:31:32.950 --> 00:31:36.530
in that clip was, I believe
Kelly's in her 30s, I think?

00:31:36.530 --> 00:31:37.363
So--
- She is, yes.

00:31:37.363 --> 00:31:39.290
- Yeah, so, that's not aging voice.

00:31:39.290 --> 00:31:41.720
That's, like, the prime of
the life for your voice.

00:31:41.720 --> 00:31:43.205
So you get in there,
the musculature develops

00:31:43.205 --> 00:31:46.460
in your 30s into your
40s, and then it starts

00:31:46.460 --> 00:31:48.410
right around in the 50s, mid-50s,

00:31:48.410 --> 00:31:51.470
it starts going downhill in
that there's muscle atrophy,

00:31:51.470 --> 00:31:54.050
there's a thinning of the
vocal folds themselves,

00:31:54.050 --> 00:31:55.810
and there's also calcification,

00:31:55.810 --> 00:31:57.430
ossification and calcification,

00:31:57.430 --> 00:31:59.283
in the vocal, in the voice box, yeah.

00:31:59.283 --> 00:32:00.830
- Hardening.
- Hardening.

00:32:00.830 --> 00:32:02.530
- Which gives you less flexibility and so

00:32:02.530 --> 00:32:04.830
there's a downgrading of technique.

00:32:04.830 --> 00:32:07.170
- It would make you less
versatile but it wouldn't

00:32:07.170 --> 00:32:08.691
still mean you can't but
some soulful elements--

00:32:08.691 --> 00:32:10.464
- Definitely not.
- In your song.

00:32:10.464 --> 00:32:11.759
- Yeah, and I think what she was saying--

00:32:11.759 --> 00:32:12.610
- Like the Tony Bennett example.

00:32:12.610 --> 00:32:13.850
- Yeah, he's one of my favorites.

00:32:13.850 --> 00:32:14.683
- He's one of my favorites.

00:32:14.683 --> 00:32:17.520
And actually, functionally
he lasted far longer

00:32:17.520 --> 00:32:19.194
than other singers of his era, so--

00:32:19.194 --> 00:32:21.087
- Because the others died.

00:32:21.087 --> 00:32:22.420
- Yeah, exactly!

00:32:23.513 --> 00:32:25.841
- Sammy's dead, they're all gone.

00:32:25.841 --> 00:32:27.999
- They're all gone!

00:32:27.999 --> 00:32:29.504
- That's one way to
outlast them, outlive them!

00:32:29.504 --> 00:32:30.954
- Martin's dead.
- Outlive them.

00:32:30.954 --> 00:32:33.566
- That is perfect, that is perfect.

00:32:33.566 --> 00:32:34.890
- Periculo's dead.

00:32:34.890 --> 00:32:36.090
- Bing Crosby, all of them!

00:32:36.090 --> 00:32:37.830
They're all dead!
- All dead!

00:32:37.830 --> 00:32:38.663
- Nice.

00:32:38.663 --> 00:32:40.894
- But if you combine the
understand a person gains

00:32:40.894 --> 00:32:44.380
all through life, that even
the sort of lack of ability

00:32:44.380 --> 00:32:46.730
they may have, or flexibility
that they may have

00:32:46.730 --> 00:32:49.320
is made up for in their
understanding of emotion,

00:32:49.320 --> 00:32:52.500
and they're more deeply
grounded in life, so that shows.

00:32:52.500 --> 00:32:53.980
- [Chuck] Hm, interesting!

00:32:53.980 --> 00:32:58.280
- So, today, as I came to learn,

00:32:58.280 --> 00:33:00.950
your singing voice, like
most things in society,

00:33:00.950 --> 00:33:03.286
can be enhanced by technology.

00:33:03.286 --> 00:33:07.883
And I asked her, I asked
Kelly, about Auto-Tune.

00:33:07.883 --> 00:33:08.716
- Uh-oh.
- Hmm.

00:33:08.716 --> 00:33:10.020
- To find out how that plays.

00:33:10.020 --> 00:33:11.201
Let's check it out.

00:33:11.201 --> 00:33:13.978
Am I allowed to ask you
in front of cameras?

00:33:13.978 --> 00:33:16.440
- Yes.
- Do you use Auto-Tune?

00:33:16.440 --> 00:33:17.273
- On records?
- Yeah.

00:33:17.273 --> 00:33:19.080
- Yes, we don't do it
live but we definitely

00:33:19.080 --> 00:33:20.040
have used it on records.

00:33:20.040 --> 00:33:21.900
- Because Auto-Tune, you
can now do it live, yeah.

00:33:21.900 --> 00:33:23.981
What you sing here comes
out on the right note--

00:33:23.981 --> 00:33:24.814
- You can do it live.

00:33:24.814 --> 00:33:27.990
There are people who do
concerts that have their vocals,

00:33:27.990 --> 00:33:30.950
yeah, because I know who they are, 'cause,

00:33:30.950 --> 00:33:32.433
I know the people that work for them.

00:33:32.433 --> 00:33:33.680
- It's just people in the
closet about their Auto-Tune.

00:33:33.680 --> 00:33:36.340
- Yeah, there are people
that Auto-Tune live.

00:33:36.340 --> 00:33:37.173
I don't do that live.

00:33:37.173 --> 00:33:39.877
I don't wanna take away
from that emotional moment.

00:33:39.877 --> 00:33:43.100
But when it's on record I don't mind.

00:33:43.100 --> 00:33:46.440
If I love a performance and I'm like, oh,

00:33:46.440 --> 00:33:48.730
I don't know if I'm gonna nail that again,

00:33:48.730 --> 00:33:50.710
or if I even try it again
and I don't nail it, like,

00:33:50.710 --> 00:33:53.090
just everything was perfect but one thing,

00:33:53.090 --> 00:33:54.655
I don't want that one thing to--

00:33:54.655 --> 00:33:55.488
- To ruin the whole recording.

00:33:55.488 --> 00:33:57.400
- To ruin the whole vibe, so I don't mind

00:33:57.400 --> 00:34:01.280
using it sparingly, but we
definitely don't use it a ton.

00:34:01.280 --> 00:34:03.000
- Because it's a major addition

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:05.667
to the musician's arsenal,
the singer's arsenal.

00:34:05.667 --> 00:34:08.100
- Yeah, and there are a lot
of cool things you can do.

00:34:08.100 --> 00:34:09.453
I mean, I know a lotta
guys in rap use them

00:34:09.453 --> 00:34:10.980
because it sounds cool, like--

00:34:10.980 --> 00:34:12.500
- Well, that's when you, okay.

00:34:12.500 --> 00:34:13.740
- Yeah, they like, over--

00:34:13.740 --> 00:34:17.160
- We did a whole program for
NOVA, back when I was host

00:34:17.160 --> 00:34:19.800
of Nova spinoff called NOVA ScienceNOW.

00:34:19.800 --> 00:34:22.789
I interviewed the guy
who invented Auto-Tune,

00:34:22.789 --> 00:34:25.707
and he was telling me
about what happens if you--

00:34:26.664 --> 00:34:27.666
- Manipulate it?
- Yeah, if you change it

00:34:27.666 --> 00:34:32.136
over the edge and then it
makes that funny distortion--

00:34:32.136 --> 00:34:33.687
- That robot kinda--
- Right, and--

00:34:33.687 --> 00:34:35.030
- Which can be cool sometimes.

00:34:35.030 --> 00:34:37.180
Because, on this, we did it
on Medicine on this album.

00:34:37.180 --> 00:34:39.550
- He made it clear that
that was not the intent.

00:34:39.550 --> 00:34:41.340
The intent is to fix your note

00:34:41.340 --> 00:34:43.890
to be the proper note,
not to dangle off the side

00:34:43.890 --> 00:34:45.890
of the waterfall where all these extra,

00:34:45.890 --> 00:34:47.070
weird noises come from.

00:34:47.070 --> 00:34:50.903
- Yes, and it's also cool, like, how we,

00:34:51.880 --> 00:34:53.230
okay, there's a song called Medicine,

00:34:53.230 --> 00:34:54.063
that's where we use it.

00:34:54.063 --> 00:34:56.453
Like, on Medicine there's
this part that goes,

00:34:57.293 --> 00:34:58.530
♪ Never get me high ♪

00:34:58.530 --> 00:34:59.500
♪ Never get me low ♪

00:34:59.500 --> 00:35:01.250
and I sing it, but then
there's this also part,

00:35:01.250 --> 00:35:04.190
they literally took my
vocal and almost, like,

00:35:04.190 --> 00:35:06.710
changed it, manipulated
it like an octave lower

00:35:06.710 --> 00:35:07.721
to where it has this like

00:35:07.721 --> 00:35:08.780
♪ Never bring me up ♪

00:35:08.780 --> 00:35:09.700
and it's like this, it sounds like a man.

00:35:09.700 --> 00:35:11.440
My mom was like, "Who's
the man on your record?"

00:35:11.440 --> 00:35:13.960
I was like, "That's my voice,
I just manipulated it."

00:35:13.960 --> 00:35:16.870
So, there's really cool
things you can do with it.

00:35:16.870 --> 00:35:20.120
I think at the end of the
day if you're not capable

00:35:20.120 --> 00:35:22.870
of doing it live, then I don't think

00:35:22.870 --> 00:35:24.113
you maybe should be doing it.

00:35:24.113 --> 00:35:26.860
That's my personal
thing, but, if it's like,

00:35:26.860 --> 00:35:29.617
gonna ruin a moment that's
really beautiful and like,

00:35:29.617 --> 00:35:31.460
you don't wanna change that moment,

00:35:31.460 --> 00:35:32.680
that doesn't bother me.

00:35:32.680 --> 00:35:36.495
- So Brian, would you
say Auto-Tune in music

00:35:36.495 --> 00:35:41.495
